// FFI as an effect — capability gating for free.
//
// Because a foreign call is a `perform` at the host seam (proposal-ffi.md
// §3.1), three properties fall out with no extra machinery:
//
//   * INTERCEPTION. A source-level handler over the module shadows the native
//     FFI handler, so a test can run against a pure mock with no library loaded.
//   * AN HONEST TYPE. The effect row `⟨sensor⟩` on the result records that the
//     call reaches foreign code — effect-polymorphic code stays honest.
//   * LEAST AUTHORITY. A world that installs no FFI handler cannot make the
//     call: the machine BLAMES the unhandled `perform` (proposal-ffi.md §3.2).
//     Blame is a *defined* halt, not a crash — the capability-denied outcome
//     made visible.
//
// This example declares a foreign `sensor` module and then calls it in a world
// with no handler installed: the call type-checks cleanly (its row is honest),
// then blames `perform-no-handler`. That single fact is the whole least-
// authority story — the most dangerous operation gandr has is denied by simply
// not answering it.

//@ mode: session
//@ expect-blame: perform-no-handler

extern "c" from "sensor" {
  def read(channel: i32) -> i64;
}

sensor.read(0i32)
